              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-pink/70 shrink-0">—</span>
              <span>
                <strong class="text-cream-warm">Token launch.</strong> With
                the assistance of BIO Protocol and the encouragement of the
                community, the DAO launched the QBIO token, making it
                transferable and publicly tradeable. The token is now
                available on Ethereum (via Uniswap) and Solana (via Raydium).
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-pink/70 shrink-0">—</span>
              <span>
                <strong class="text-cream-warm">Community building.</strong>
                Through ups and downs, many people, including Geoff Anders,
                Kyra Gardner, Jennilyn Gaitan, Stanley Bishop, Cryptonurse,
                Holypanetone, TheWeb5sASI, Royalty, DePaul, Kenichi, iFun,
                Scagria, Nonye, Michael_019, Timmygram and others, helped to
                grow and build the community. Through that work, there are now
                more than 50 people actively involved in the DAO.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-pink/70 shrink-0">—</span>
              <span>
                <strong class="text-cream-warm">Organizational setup.</strong>
                The DAO also set up its own governance structure and
                committees, selected committee leaders, divided tasks, and
                took steps to ensure organizational efficiency.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-pink/70 shrink-0">—</span>
              <span>
                <strong class="text-cream-warm">First major grant.</strong>
                The Quantum Biology DAO gave its first major grant, of 500 ETH
                (~$1.3 million at the time) to the Quantum Biology Institute
                to support its foundational work in the field of quantum
                biology. QBI’s major project is creating a microscope capable
                of detecting quantum states inside cells and room temperature,
                and also applying magnetic fields to probe and interact with
                those quantum states. It’s very exciting work, and it is only
                possible now because of the DAO’s support.
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>New Priorities</h3>
          <p>The DAO has several top priorities at the moment.</p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-cream-warm">A new scientific granting process</strong>
            — The DAO needs to set up a granting process that will guide its
            future grants to scientists who will advance the field of quantum
            biology. Researchers and academics are thoroughly dissatisfied
            with the granting system that currently exists. We have the
            opportunity to improve on it!
          </p>
          <p class="text-cream/60 text-[13px]">The entire DAO will cover this.</p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-cream-warm">Health effects from weak magnetic fields</strong>
            — The biggest and most immediate point of contact between people’s
            everyday lives and quantum biology are the potential health
            effects of weak magnetic fields. These are frequently ignored
            because the fields are thought to be too weak; however, there are
            quantum effects that are strong precisely when the fields are
            weak. These may be effects that researchers and government bodies
            looking at health effects are unaware of.
          </p>
          <p class="text-cream/60 text-[13px]">The Science committee will cover this.</p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-cream-warm">DAO website refresh</strong> — The
            DAO’s <a
              href="http://quantumbiology.xyz"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>website</a
            > is sorely out of date. It was current in December 2024 when the
            initial fundraise took place. However, a lot has happened in the
            months since then.
          </p>
          <p class="text-cream/60 text-[13px]">The Communications committee will cover this.</p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-cream-warm">First patent and tokenized IP</strong>
            — One reason scientists do not look into quantum biology is that
            they do not know how to apply magnetic fields to biological
            samples. The first step in solving this is to create an
            easy-to-use adjustable magnetic ring that biologists can use in
            experiments. This can be patented and then tokenized, with
            ownership of the tokens determining licensing rights. Getting a
            patent will demonstrate commercial opportunities within quantum
            biology and tokenization will demonstrate a practical use case
            for blockchain technology.
          </p>
          <p class="text-cream/60 text-[13px]">
            This is the Product committee’s first project. See <a
              href="/roadmap"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>the Quantum Biology DAO’s product roadmap</a
            >.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-cream-warm">Treasury management strategy</strong>
            — Thus far, the DAO has simply held ETH and staked some of that
            ETH. It is important to develop a new treasury management plan in
            communication with the DAO’s stakeholders, designed to optimize
            the DAO’s management of risk.
          </p>
          <p class="text-cream/60 text-[13px]">This is the Treasury committee’s aim.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

