      <!-- AFFILIATES -->
      <article id="affiliates" class={`${bentoCard} scroll-mt-28`}>
        <EyebrowLabel accent="pink">Affiliates</EyebrowLabel>
        <h2 class={`${bentoHeading} mb-8`}>
          The <em class="italic text-pink/90">affiliates</em>.
        </h2>
        <div class={`${bodyText} max-w-3xl`}>
          <p>
            The Quantum Biology DAO is affiliated with a number of groups,
            movements, and organizations, including many similar DAOs. The
            most important of these are the Quantum Biology Ecosystem, the
            Decentralized Science movement, and the BIO ecosystem.
          </p>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>Quantum Biology Ecosystem</h3>
          <p>
            The <a
              href="http://quantumbiology.eco"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>Quantum Biology Ecosystem</a
            > is a network of three organizations:
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-pink/70 shrink-0">—</span><span>Quantum Biology Institute</span></li>
            <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-pink/70 shrink-0">—</span><span>Quantum Biology DAO</span></li>
            <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-pink/70 shrink-0">—</span><span>Quantum Biology Incubator</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The <a
              href="http://quantumbiology.org"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>Quantum Biology Institute</a
            > is a 501(c)(3) non-profit organization located in California. It
            is devoted to performing the basic research needed to lay the
            foundation for the field of quantum biology. It has a lab in Los
            Angeles, where it is building a microscope that will allow it to
            examine quantum states inside cells at room temperature. It also
            has a smaller lab hosted at the J. Craig Venter Institute in
            Maryland, where microbiology experiments are performed to better
            understand the effects of weak magnetic fields in biology.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Quantum Biology Institute was originally founded by Clarice
            Aiello in 2024. It is now led by Anna Stillwell, with support from
            the team, which includes Alessandro Lodesani, Mike Montague,
            Jennilyn Gaitain, and others.
          </p>
          <p>
            The <a
              href="http://quantumbiology.xyz"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>Quantum Biology DAO</a
            > is the organization described in this document! It was conceived
            in April 2024 and debuted in November 2024. It differs from the
            Institute in that it involves many different people, is officially
            headquartered in Switzerland rather than Los Angeles, and is
            focused on the translational efforts needed to bridge from the
            basic science to commercialization, rather than simply on the
            basic science.
          </p>
          <p>
            During the initial DAO fundraise, Geoff Anders and Clarice Aiello
            pitched the entire Quantum Biology Ecosystem, as a
            three-organization structure needed to build a new industry. The
            Quantum Biology DAO was explained as the second of three
            organizations, with the intention that half of the money raised
            would go to the Quantum Biology Institute and half to the rest of
            the field. This was explained with the proviso that, since final
            decision making power at the DAO rests with the token holders, all
            allocations of money would be subject to the token holders’
            approval.
          </p>
          <p>
            The <a
              href="http://quantumbiology.inc"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>Quantum Biology Incubator</a
            > [website is being reposted] is a new organization, ready to be
            founded, which will help equip startups in the quantum biology
            space with the scientific knowledge, cutting edge technology, and
            business know-how needed to succeed. According to the Quantum
            Biology Ecosystem <a
              href="/roadmap"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>roadmap</a
            >, the Incubator is slated to launch in mid-2025:
          </p>
          <p>
            In order to keep the three organizations distinct but aligned,
            each was given its own overlapping but related mission. Also, it
            was planned that the Quantum Biology Institute and the Quantum
            Biology DAO would both receive a portion of equity from the
            Quantum Biology Incubator. If everything goes according to plan,
            every successful exit of an incubated company will feed money back
            into the system, helping to fund the Institute and the DAO. In
            this way, it becomes possible for quantum biology to become a
            completely self-sustaining sector.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Quantum Biology Ecosystem website also contains a large number
            of <a
              href="http://quantumbiology.eco#briefs"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>briefs</a
            >, designed to teach people about the relationship between quantum
            biology and other fields. These include:
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            {
              briefs.map((b) => (
                <li class="flex gap-3">
                  <span class="text-pink/70 shrink-0">—</span>
                  <a
                    href={b.href}
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class={lk}
                  >
                    {b.label}
                  </a>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>Decentralized Science (“DeSci”)</h3>
          <p>
            Decentralized Science, or “DeSci,” is a movement that has existed
            at least since 2018. Originally beginning with elements of the
            Open Science movement, as well as the advent of preprint servers,
            Decentralized Science moved onto the blockchain, with many groups
            launching crypto-native organizations seeking to improve science
            through the use of blockchain technology.
          </p>
          <p>
            One important aspect of DeSci has been the adoption of DAOs. “DAO”
            stands for “Decentralized Autonomous Organization,” a special type
            of organization that should be made possible by the blockchain.
            Over the years, different teams have experimented with different
            degrees of decentralization and autonomy, with the general
            consensus now being that decentralization and autonomy are good
            and that a progressive move towards each over time is the best
            overall strategy.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Quantum Biology DAO is a member of the Decentralized Science
            movement. We have participated in several DeSci conferences,
            including DeSci Bangkok and DeSci London, and have attended DeSci
            events at larger crypto conferences including ETH Denver. We
            expect that DAO community members will continue to participate in
            and contribute to the DeSci movement, at least in the form of
            participation, if not (eventually) in the form of leadership.
          </p>
          <p>
            As a DAO, the Quantum Biology DAO has the goal of progressively
            moving towards greater decentralization and autonomy. That means
            that the DAO is aiming to cause power at the DAO to be held by
            more and more people over time and for the DAO to operate in an
            increasingly automatic way over time. Premature decentralization
            and premature automation are two major causes of DAO failure, so
            we are being careful. But these are ideals we take seriously and
            believe we are making progress towards.
          </p>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>BIO Ecosystem</h3>
          <p>
            One of the most prominent organizations in DeSci right now is <a
              href="https://www.bio.xyz/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>BIO Protocol</a
            >, the organization affiliated with the <a
              href="https://coinmarketcap.com/currencies/bio/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>BIO</a
            > token. BIO Protocol incubates and launches DeSci DAOs, which
            then become part of its ecosystem. The BIO Ecosystem now contains
            many DAOs, including <a href="https://www.vitadao.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={lk}>VitaDAO</a> (longevity),
            <a href="https://www.hairdao.xyz/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={lk}>HairDAO</a> (baldness),
            <a href="https://www.valleydao.bio/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={lk}>ValleyDAO</a> (synthetic biology),
            <a href="https://www.athenadao.co/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={lk}>AthenaDAO</a> (women’s health),
            <a href="https://www.cryodao.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={lk}>CryoDAO</a> (cryopreservation),
            <a href="https://www.psydao.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={lk}>PsyDAO</a> (psychedelics),
            <a href="https://www.cerebrumdao.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={lk}>Cerebrum DAO</a> (brain health),
            <a href="https://www.mycodao.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={lk}>MycoDAO</a> (fungi),
            <a href="https://www.spinedao.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={lk}>SpineDAO</a> (spine health),
            <a href="https://www.longcovidlabs.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={lk}>Long Covid Labs</a> (long covid),
            <a href="https://www.curetopia.xyz/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={lk}>Curetopia</a> (rare diseases),
            and, of course, the <a href="http://quantumbiology.xyz" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={lk}>Quantum Biology DAO</a> (quantum biology).
          </p>
          <p>
            BIO Protocol arose from an earlier organization, <a
              href="https://www.molecule.to/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>Molecule</a
            >, which has pioneered different means to put science and
            intellectual property onto the blockchain. There are now a network
            of organizations with the BIO ecosystem working together to
            advance the cause of science through the DeSci movement and web3.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- HISTORY -->
      <article id="history" class={`${bentoCard} scroll-mt-28`}>
        <EyebrowLabel accent="pink">History</EyebrowLabel>
        <h2 class={`${bentoHeading} mb-8`}>
          A pretty <em class="italic text-pink/90">wild ride</em> so far.
        </h2>
        <div class={`${bodyText} max-w-3xl`}>
          <p>
            Despite its relative short history, the Quantum Biology DAO has
            had a pretty wild ride so far! Read on to learn about the ups and
            downs, as well as accomplishments and next steps.
          </p>
          <p>As a summary, the history of the DAO can be broken down as follows:</p>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            {
              historySummary.map((h) => (
                <li class="flex gap-3">
                  <span class="text-pink/70 shrink-0">—</span>
                  <span>{h}</span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>Pre-History 📜</h3>
          <p>
            For several years, Clarice Aiello had been advocating for
            resources and attention to go to the field of quantum biology.
            With her training as a quantum engineer and experience in quantum
            sensing, she realized that it was likely that biological cells
            were engaging in quantum sensing also — but that very few people
            had realized this.
          </p>
          <p>
            Why did few people believe that cells were engaged in quantum
            sensing? Most scientists were unfamiliar with quantum physics and
            were scared of the mathematics. Those that did study quantum
            phenomena usually did so under very controlled circumstances,
            frequently at very low temperatures. These scientists, including
            those working on quantum computers, found it extremely difficult
            to preserve quantum states for long periods of time, and so it was
            believed that quantum states could not exist for long enough in
            the chaotic environment of the cell to be biologically relevant.
          </p>
          <p>
            However, there was evidence that cells were somehow preserving and
            relying on quantum states, despite being very complicated and
            existing at room temperature. This evidence came primarily in the
            form of effects from weak magnetic fields. Many organisms
            experienced effects from weak magnetic fields, including fields as
            weak as the one produced by the Earth. These effects had been
            observed for decades and—this was the important part—in most cases
            there weren’t good classical (meaning, non-quantum) explanations
            for how the effects came about.
          </p>
          <p>
            Rather than argue, Clarice set out to build a microscope capable
            of looking inside cells and seeing whether quantum states actually
            did last for long enough to be biologically relevant. She created
            a research plan and tried to raise money. While she was successful
            to a degree, it was hard to find the institutional support she
            needed, so she ended up leaving academia and striking out on her
            own.
          </p>
          <p>
            Just before leaving, Clarice happened to reach out to an
            organization called Leverage (<a
              href="http://leverage.institute"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>leverage.institute</a
            >). Leverage specializes in helping scientific fields that are
            stuck to become unstuck. In 2023, she had several conversations
            with the Leverage CEO, Geoff Anders. Geoff became convinced that
            quantum biology was an extremely important field and that it could
            be unstuck with the right effort.
          </p>
          <p>
            In April 2024, Geoff and Leverage team decided to go all in on
            quantum biology. Geoff and Clarice joined forces, and together
            they reached out to everyone in their combined networks, from
            contacts in government and defense to tech and crypto billionaires
            to colleagues at NIH and NASA, and more. One of the groups they
            reached out to was BIO Protocol, where Geoff and Clarice met Paul
            Kohlhaas, BIO’s CEO, and James Sinka, BIO’s Science Lead.
          </p>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>Incubation 🌱</h3>
          <p>
            Initially, Geoff and Clarice were wary. This was crypto, after
            all. Clarice did not have any experience with crypto. Geoff had
            previously advised a crypto project and thus had some familiarity,
            but neither had previously encountered BIO or “decentralized
            science.” The people seemed genuine, however, and the ideas seemed
            excellent. So Geoff and Clarice began developing the plan for the
            Quantum Biology DAO.
          </p>
          <p>
            The process of incubation involved developing a bunch of ideas and
            materials which would be used as part of the DAO fundraise.
            Importantly, Geoff and Clarice were trying to raise between $5-20
            million for quantum biology, and were still trying to raise money
            from government sources, philanthropists, and others during this
            time. Peter Thiel had suggested he would put in $5 million,
            conditional on another $15 million being raised. Geoff and Clarice
            thought they could raise $10 million from philanthropists and $5
            million more from the DAO, which would be enough to unlock the
            last $5 million.
          </p>
          <p>
            While speaking to so many parties, Geoff developed the design for
            the Quantum Biology Ecosystem, which included three organizations
            and a place for anyone with money to contribute. The Institute
            could raise money from government sources and philanthropists. The
            DAO could raise money from crypto investors. The Incubator could
            raise money from VCs. Together, the three organizations made it
            possible to speak to everyone interested and turn no one away.
          </p>
          <p>
            Not everything went according to plan, however. One government
            source, which had credibly indicated a desire to put in several
            million dollars, had its own funding reallocated. One
            philanthropist, who had indicated serious interest initially,
            vanished near the end. It was too early for VCs and the best
            philanthropic opportunity wasn’t closing. It was now November 2024
            and Clarice’s money had run out. Geoff had scrounged up enough
            money to keep the Institute running and fund the Tadpole
            Experiment, an experiment which showed that biological effects
            from weak magnetic fields were real. The science was good, but
            where was the money?
          </p>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>Debut 🕺</h3>
          <p>
            The team at BIO Protocol strongly encouraged Geoff and Clarice to
            travel to Thailand for DeSci Bangkok and Devcon 7. The two decided
            to go. On stage at DeSci Bangkok, Geoff presented the Quantum
            Biology DAO for the first time. Crypto VCs were interested. Geoff
            and Clarice were invited to a special Binance event. Things were
            on a knife’s edge. Should they launch the DAO, even though the
            rest of the money had not been secured? The choice may have been
            DeSci or nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            At the Binance event, two special guests were announced: Vitalik
            Buterin, creator of Ethereum, and CZ, founder of Binance. Both
            gave the nod to DeSci. This was an important area, they said.
            Geoff and Clarice each ended up with a moment to talk with Vitalik
            and Clarice spoke briefly with CZ. Not enough time, however, to
            really lay out the vision.
          </p>
          <p>
            As Geoff and Clarice returned to the US, DeSci was exploding as a
            sector. Tons of interest was pouring in. The original plan with
            BIO had been to launch the Quantum Biology DAO in April 2025. It
            was now December 2024. Geoff and Clarice pulled in Alessandro
            Lodesani, the other scientist at the Quantum Biology Institute,
            and the three moved up the timeline and took the plunge… which
            meant launching immediately.
          </p>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>Token Auction 🪙</h3>
          <p>
            From December 4 through December 16, Geoff, Clarice, and Ale
            worked non-stop. Tons of materials had already been created.
            Oliver Carefull, at Leverage, had turned Clarice’s research plan
            and Geoff’s vision for the Quantum Biology Ecosystem into a
            compelling roadmap. The Leverage team had also taken scientific
            material from Clarice and turned it into more than a dozen briefs
            that related quantum biology to many other fields. Si Mclennan,
            sponsored by BIO and in collaboration with Oliver, produced four
            beautiful websites, one each for the Institute, DAO, and
            Incubator, and one of the Quantum Biology Ecosystem itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            Geoff and Clarice, now joined by Ale, did non-stop X spaces at all
            hours, public interviews with scientists, conversations with
            crypto VCs, and produced new materials essentially non-stop.
            Materials included the Quantum Biology DAO <a
              href="http://bit.ly/qbiodaovision"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>Vision Paper</a
            >, <a
              href="http://bit.ly/qbiodaoroadmap"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>Roadmap</a
            >, and <a
              href="http://bit.ly/qbiodaodeck"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>Deck</a
            >, as well as a growing list of advisors and endorsements,
            including a key endorsement at the last minute from a member of
            Merck Group, who wrote that Merck Group was interested in quantum
            biology — of the exact sort that we were working on.
          </p>
          <p>
            The auction, which began on December 4, had a minimum threshold of
            $500 thousand. Had anything less been raised, the auction would
            have failed and all money would have been refunded. We hit $500
            thousand within seventeen minutes. By the end of the first day, we
            were at just over $750 thousand, which certainly wasn’t bad. The
            team, however, had already decided that we needed to raise between
            $2 million and $2.5 million if the project would actually be able
            to proceed. Quantum instrumentation is expensive, which was one
            big reason this had never been done before.
          </p>
          <p>
            At that point, people were still allowed to retract their bids.
            The total amount in went up and down, then, for the next week,
            hovering between $750 thousand and $900 thousand. After day 8,
            however, the amount pledged in bids began to rise. We hit $1
            million by the weekend, and $1.4 million twelve hours before the
            close. In the last twelve hours, or really the last four, the
            auction went crazy. Bids started pouring in, and in the last few
            minutes we hit an asymptote. The auction closed and we had raised
            $6.8 million.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where did the $6.8 million come from? On a technical level, the
            auction worked by allowing people to place bids. A weighted
            average of bids was then calculated, a single closing price
            determined, and all bids below that price rejected and refunded.
            All bids that were not rejected then paid the closing price. Of
            $8.2 million bid, $1.4 million of bids were rejected, leaving $6.8
            million for us. It also so happened that ETH had spiked at that
            exact moment, putting it just below its three-year high.
          </p>
          <p>
            In terms of who contributed, it’s hard to say. The blockchain is
            pseudonymous. A plot of the successful bids, however, was like the
            function f(x) = 1 / x. In other words, it was a smooth curve. A
            small number of large bids, a medium number of medium-sized bids,
            a large number of small bids. It seemed like everyone was on our
            side and agreed: now was the time for quantum biology.
          </p>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>Initial Setup 🏗️</h3>
          <p>
            After the auction, the team was exhausted. It was also the
            holidays, so Geoff, Clarice, and Ale took a week off. Little did
            they realize, crypto never sleeps. It’s 24/7. By the time the
            three logged back in, the community Discord was full of people
            clamoring for the token to be launched. We had raised money
            through the auction, but we hadn’t made the new QBIO token, which
            we had auctioned off, transferable or tradeable.
          </p>
          <p>
            The team quickly moved to rectify the situation. They apologized
            for the delay, created the DAO’s governance system, and then made
            two proposals, one to make QBIO transferable and tradeable on the
            Ethereum blockchain, and the other to bridge to Solana, making
            QBIO accessible there as well. The proposals passed by a wide
            margin with many votes in favor.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Ethereum launch happened soon after. The Solana launch was
            delayed due to technical difficulties from one of BIO’s providers.
            The token eventually became available on the Solana blockchain in
            March. In the meantime, the DAO co-founders created the DAO’s
            committees (aka “working groups) and passed a budget for the DAO.
            They also began proposing monthly grants to tide over the Quantum
            Biology Institute, where Clarice and Ale were working. This was
            enough to set up the DAO in a basic sense, though, of course,
            trouble was coming.
          </p>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>Token Launch 🚀</h3>
          <p>
            In the auction, 15% of the total supply of QBIO had been sold.
            Many people now had QBIO burning a hole in their pockets. QBIO,
            one will recall, is a governance token. It is a utility token
            meant to be used to vote on what the Quantum Biology DAO does.
            Nevertheless, people may have reasons to want to sell their QBIO
            tokens and do something else with their money. That’s perfectly
            legitimate as well.
          </p>
          <p>
            For whatever reason, there was substantial pressure to make the
            QBIO token tradeable. BIO had not yet built infrastructure to
            ensure that new DeSci DAO tokens would be available for public
            trade right away. So holders of QBIO had been waiting for almost a
            month with the proposal to make QBIO tradeable passed.
          </p>
          <p>
            As the date of the token launch approached, advisors to the
            Quantum Biology DAO became nervous. Some reached out and suggested
            in strong terms that launching the token now would be a mistake.
            The co-founders of the DAO consulted an independent advisor and
            heard the same message: now was a bad time to launch. Was it
            macroeconomic conditions? Was it the fact that the TRUMP token was
            set to launch essentially the same day?
          </p>
          <p>
            The team quickly made a new proposal to delay the launch, but the
            community voted it down. There was now no choice. The team, with
            BIO’s technical assistance, unlocked the QBIO token and made it
            available for trade on Uniswap, a decentralized exchange on the
            Ethereum blockchain. QBIO began trading on January 17th, 2025.
          </p>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>Two Crashes ☄️</h3>
          <p>
            The Quantum Biology DAO team then experienced two crashes. The
            first was the QBIO token. It was initially listed at $0.03588,
            down from its auction price of $0.04583, since ETH had come back
            down. It then dropped to $0.005183 before recovering to $0.02094
            and then going into a steady decline.
          </p>
          <p>
            At its lowest point in January, QBIO was down 85.6% from its
            listing price and 88.7% from the original auction price. Other
            biology-related DAO tokens at the time <a
              href="https://dexscreener.com/ethereum/0x9c4980635d980611cecdbca74773e240ffa44240"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>also</a
            > <a
              href="https://dexscreener.com/ethereum/0xcab7d1c2777488b85ed301c3944cdbd6bb30bf72"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class={lk}>crashed</a
            >, so it may have been market conditions or DeSci falling out of
            the limelight. In fact, QBIO did better than many other tokens,
            but that didn’t mean the crash was good.
          </p>
          <p>
            The QBIO token crash was then followed by another crash: Ethereum.
            ETH had been at nearly $4,000 when the QBIO auction closed. By the
            end of February and into March 2025, ETH was on its way down, and
            would eventually bottom around $1,400. That may or may not have
            impacted the QBIO token, but it certainly impacted the Quantum
            Biology DAO’s cash reserves, which were still being held
            exclusively in ETH.
          </p>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>Voting Battle ⚔️</h3>
          <p>
            Right on the heels of two crashes, the DAO had a huge clash over
            governance. This was, in some sense, all part of the plan: DAO
            governance is real and there were competing visions for the future
            of the DAO.
          </p>
          <p>
            The battle was sparked by a proposal to fund the Quantum Biology
            Institute, which had been circulating a proposed annual budget,
            covering all of its instrument, personnel, location, and other
            costs. The Institute team discussed it with their partners and the
            DAO discussed it with the community. The important point about the
            annual budget was that the price tag was high: $3.4 million.
          </p>
          <p>
            A proposal to fund the QBI budget passed from drafting (Phase 1)
            to discussion and voting on Common (Phase 2). It looked like the
            proposal was going to pass, but then a large number of negative
            votes materialized as if out of nowhere. Spam filled the comments
            and there was a race between positive and negative votes, with
            negative votes winning out in the end.
          </p>
          <p>
            Next, a proposal to fund QBI just for one month, as a tideover
            budget, also failed, again with a large number of negative votes
            and this time with a DAO community member being impersonated in
            the comments. Members of the DAO tried to identify any person who
            had voted negatively, since everyone who had spoken up in earlier
            discussions was in favor of funding the Institute. No one was
            identified, and there <em>had</em> been signs of irregularities in
            the voting, so the DAO voted to deactivate Phase 2 for repair.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the next few months, proposals went straight from Phase 1
            (drafting on Discord) to Phase 3 (token voting on Snapshot).
          </p>
          <p>
            During this time, two different visions of DAO governance were
            presented. One side, led by Geoff Anders and Alessandro Lodesani,
            proposed adding a token gate of 10,000 QBIO to Phase 2 to make it
            harder for people to vote with multiple accounts, and sending
            known community participants 10,000 QBIO from the DAO treasury so
            they would be able to vote in Phase 2. The other side, led by
            Clarice Aiello and the BIO team, argued in favor of simplifying
            governance by removing Phase 2 and token-gating (i.e., having a
            token ownership requirement) for participating in drafting on the
            Discord.
          </p>
          <p>
            After lengthy discussion, the issue was put to a vote in the
            Discord. The first proposal won out by a margin of 15-8. It then
            passed Phase 3. Phase 2 was reinstantiated with a token gate of
            10,000 QBIO and a list of 40 community participants were given the
            option to receive 10,000 QBIO from the DAO treasury.
          </p>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>Recovery 💪</h3>
          <p>
            Two token crashes and a voting battle took up a bunch of
            attention. The DAO, however, bounced back. Starting in April and
            then going into May, ETH began its recovery, reaching back to
            $1,800 and then $2,500. The QBIO price also began to recover, with
            sequences of purchases at one point leading the QBIO price to
            triple.
          </p>
          <p>
            A long discussion about how to fix Phase 2 of governance occurred,
            with one side arguing in favor of simpler, more centralized
            governance and the other side arguing for a token gate. In May
            2025, the token gate side won out, Phase 2 was reinstalled, and
            DAO governance repaired. The source of the Twitter hack was also
            discovered and everyone received a primer on taking better
            security measures.
          </p>
          <p>
            Throughout, morale at the Quantum Biology DAO remained very high.
            One key contributor was the fact that the community stayed
            positive. Maybe all the speculators left when the token crashed
            initially. Maybe the people who want to stick around for trivia
            and fun DAO mascots are people who don’t like FUD. Whatever the
            reason, the community stayed positive and the DAO overall stayed
            confident and ready to win.
          </p>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>Setup Complete ☑️</h3>
          <p>
            The DAO was now started to mature. DAO governance had been
            upgraded. New committees were added and committee leaders
            selected. Everyone was ready to go.
          </p>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>First Major Grant 💰</h3>
          <p>
            In June 2025, the DAO made its first major grant. It had been
            funding the Quantum Biology Institute month-to-month from February
            through June, but now voted to set aside 500 ETH for the Institute
            to draw on as needed. This was one of the two main reasons the DAO
            had been founded in the first place.
          </p>

          <h3 class={h3Class}>Next Steps 🛣️</h3>
          <p>
            What’s next? The DAO’s next steps, after having funded the Quantum
            Biology Institute, is to figure out what else is necessary to
            advance the rest of the field. There are instruments to build,
            other research avenues to fund, and more. The DAO is ready, the
            future is bright — and may the quantum be with you!
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

